#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 480.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120636} :activity/id #uuid "3cf7c572-6107-5fa5-b01f-b66c67910b92" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050092 :topic/id #uuid "2cd491c7-194b-5698-adc2-6e400c2dadcc" :topic/math-academy-id 1769} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120636 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186121025 :tutorial/content "The minor of the entry $a_{ij}$ in a $n\\times n$ matrix is the determinant that we get when the rows and columns containing $a_{ij}$ are removed.\n\nFor example, suppose that we wish to find the minor of $a_{32}$ in the following matrix:\n\n<div class=\"matrix-operands matrix-operands-stacked\">\n<div>$A=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 2 & 5 & 12 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 0 & -5 \\\\ -5 & 1 & 3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$</div>\n</div>\n\nTo get the minor of $a_{\\color{red}{3}\\color{blue}{2}}$, we remove the $\\color{red}3$rd row and $\\color{blue}2$nd column:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}2 & ∗ & 2 & 3 \\\\ 4 & ∗ & 5 & 12 \\\\ ∗ & -2 & ∗ & ∗ \\\\ -5 & ∗ & 3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nSo, the minor of $a_{32}$, denoted $M_{32}$, is \n\n$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 5 & 12 \\\\ -5 & 3 & 0\\end{vmatrix}\n$$\n\nWe can now calculate the value of $M_{32}$ by evaluating the determinant using previously discussed methods." :tutorial/id #uuid "c271b45c-6e89-58a5-b33f-72802abba7b7" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3020 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "3ac60505-52cd-5e2b-9f9b-561f8de1daf6" :step/math-academy-id 16253 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120637}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120637 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119781 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119595 :question/id #uuid "3545b168-5226-5f47-ac0d-3ff5509e866e" :question/math-academy-id "e-4447"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ecdc6a5a-5aba-5547-97aa-db3cd2e90ae6" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048146}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120509} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120510} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265949} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265979} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265981} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388174} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186390791} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186391944} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186393091} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186393098}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Minor in a NxN Matrix"} :step/id #uuid "01259f05-223f-5006-b44d-adea1cc4f258" :step/math-academy-id 16255 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120741}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120638 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119782 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119596 :question/id #uuid "8e61e639-f165-5157-91c0-e9a6faf734a6" :question/math-academy-id "e-4448"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "58148550-e41f-5df0-80dc-e783619cf87d" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120400} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120505} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266007} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266008} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266009} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302373} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323744} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326843} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186364112} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367324} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186368489} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388188} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186392455}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Cofactor in a NxN Matrix"} :step/id #uuid "257dfffc-b40d-5d48-bc3c-1a5ab49ac70c" :step/math-academy-id 16256 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120740}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120639 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119783 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119597 :question/id #uuid "3e091496-053c-59fb-90d6-d8fb89ea6004" :question/math-academy-id "e-4449"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "055b3809-f9aa-5dcc-8c1a-b0eb71020d7e" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120207} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120401} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266045} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266046} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266048} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266049} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302331} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302372} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323745} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323746} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186368541}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Determinant of a NxN Matrix Using Cofactor Expansion"} :step/id #uuid "bb444c11-cfd5-5490-9e3d-21530e787e2d" :step/math-academy-id 16257} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120740 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186121059 :tutorial/content "One way to find the determinant of a $3\\!\\times\\! 3$ matrix $A$ is to take the alternating sum of the entries of the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\det(A) = {\\color{red}a_{11}}{\\color{blue}M_{11}} - {\\color{red}a_{12}}{\\color{blue}M_{12}} + {\\color{red}a_{13}}{\\color{blue}M_{13}}\n$$\n\nRemember that we had to change the sign of the second product. When we multiply a minor of an entry by the respective sign in the formula like this, we get the cofactor of the entry. The cofactors in the above formula were ${\\color{blue}M_{11}},$ $-{\\color{blue}M_{12}},$ and ${\\color{blue}M_{13}}.$\n\nUsing this idea, we can write the formula for the determinant of a $3\\!\\times\\! 3$ matrix $A$ in terms of cofactors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\det(A) = {\\color{red}a_{11}}{\\color{blue}C_{11}} + {\\color{red}a_{12}}{\\color{blue}C_{12}} + {\\color{red}a_{13}}{\\color{blue}C_{13}}\n$$\n\nThis is called the **cofactor expansion** of the determinant across the first row.\n\nIn fact, we can use a similar method of multiplying the entries of the top row of the matrix by their respective cofactors to find the determinant for any $n\\!\\times\\! n$ matrix $A$ when $n \\ge 3$. The formula is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & ={\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}{a}_{1j}{C}_{1j} \\\\  & ={a}_{11}{C}_{11}+{a}_{12}{C}_{12}+⋯+{a}_{1n}{C}_{1n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's see how to use this formula in the next example." :tutorial/id #uuid "d83d34fa-1d87-5510-bfd8-8673b2bd79cf" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3801 :tutorial/title "Calculating Determinants Using Cofactors"} :step/id #uuid "336a8071-c9c8-57a3-9cb3-7788855b7df7" :step/math-academy-id 21756 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120639}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120741 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186121060 :tutorial/content "In general, for any $n\\times n$ matrix $A,$ the **cofactor** $C_{ij}$ of the entry $\\color{red}a_{ij}$ is\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}C_{ij}}=(-1)^{i+j}{\\color{blue}M_{ij}}.\n$$\n\nCofactors are a useful tool in helping us to compute determinants of larger matrices. Before we learn how, let's get some practice at computing cofactors." :tutorial/id #uuid "6c2b7774-d437-5cf9-812f-4d6ad8dc9bf3" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3802 :tutorial/title "Calculating a Cofactor of a NxN Matrix"} :step/id #uuid "0e7f0690-9996-5a0c-b0cc-5f9200df7c10" :step/math-academy-id 21772 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120638}}] :activity/title "The Determinant of an NxN Matrix" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186119991}]}
